{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>To the door or to the doorpost.</b> Of the gate of the court, as an example: 'and you shall bring that man out to your gate' — in order to publicize the matter before the eyes of all passersby, that he enslaves himself for a Canaanite maidservant, and that he must serve his service until the Jubilee." "<b>Or to the doorpost.</b> Rashi explained: Scripture compared the door to the doorpost — just as the doorpost is fixed (standing), etc. And the Jerusalem Targum proves this: 'to the door that is next to the doorpost.' And if you wish, the word 'or' means 'in the place where,' that is: and his master shall bring him to the door, which is fixed next to the doorpost, and that is what is meant by 'fixed.' And similarly: 'or it be known that the ox was wont to gore.' 'Or he strengthens himself in his stronghold.' Here Rashi explained: at the time 'when I passed over the lintel' — 'and I said: for to Me the children of Israel are servants.' And HazZL said that this verse was not said until Sinai. And Rashi further explained: if he sold himself — the ear that heard 'for to Me the children of Israel are servants,' and this one went and acquired a master for himself. And if you ask: but is not the beginning of his servitude, when he acquired a master for himself, already obligated for this reason to be bored through? Therefore the text reads 'and this one went and acquires' — meaning, at the end of his servitude, when he wishes to go out free, and he goes and again acquires for himself a master a second time — he is pierced with an awl. And what Rashi explained here, 'an ear that heard at Sinai: for to Me the children of Israel are servants' — we learn that this passage was included in the portion of Behar, as I explained above, since 'for to Me the children of Israel are servants' was not said until after the giving of the Torah. And Rashi further explained: the ear that heard at Sinai 'you shall not steal' — this is the reading לא תגנובו (plural). And some have the reading לא תגנב (singular). For לא תגנב refers to one who steals persons and is liable to the death penalty and is not sold; but לא תגנבו refers to one who steals money, and if he has no means to pay, he is sold for his theft. The reason it specified לא תגנבו rather than 'a robber' and other transgressions is that he feared the authority of the blessed Holy One more, unlike the robber and other transgressions. As our Sages taught: the disciples asked Rabban Yohanan ben Zakkai, why did the Torah deal more strictly with a thief than with a robber? He said to them: this one equated the honor of the servant to the honor of his Master, and that one did not equate the honor of the servant to the honor of his Master. As if he made, as it were, the eye below as though it does not see, and the ear below as though it does not hear. <b>And his master shall bore through.</b> So that if he flees from his master, the boring of his ear will testify about him that he is his slave — when it will be recognized as matching the boring of the door. And also the master will not be able to contest another slave, since the boring of the slave and of the door will not be identical, for one will be high and the other low." "<b>And his master shall bore through.</b> The reason the blessed Holy One commanded to bore through a Hebrew slave is so that people not be mistaken about him and say he is a Canaanite, when they see that he is worked so hard." "<b>His ear.</b> And he will not be able to say: I injured myself there." "<b>His ear.</b> For so is the way of human beings, that they do something improper, and from this they learned to do so to thieves." "<b>With an awl.</b> I heard a midrash: HaShem said: I decreed upon Israel a servitude of four hundred years, and afterward I advanced the end for their sake and shortened the servitude — and this one went and enslaved himself; let him be pierced with a tool whose numerical value is four hundred — and that is מרצע." "<b>And his servant.</b> Not the son and not the daughter. Hence they said: a Hebrew slave serves the son but does not serve the daughter. If he was pierced, he does not serve the son and does not serve the daughter." "<b>לעלם.</b> Rashi explains: until the Jubilee. Or is it nothing but לעולם in its plain sense, etc. And if you should say that this is so — why do I need 'והתנחלתם אותם'? Now if a Hebrew slave serves forever, a Canaanite slave — all the more so! Rather, one may answer: this is what it says: 'or is it nothing but לעולם in its plain sense' — all the days of the master's life, which are his 'עולם'; that is, until the end of his life, which are seventy years, since presumably he is now twenty-five, so that fifty years of service makes seventy. Therefore it writes אל משפחתו תשובו — this tells that if the Jubilee arrives before the master's death, he goes free. And further, Rashi explains: this tells that fifty years are called עולם. And so we find with Samuel the Ramathite, whose mother said in the holy spirit: וישב שם עד עולם — and when she brought him he was two years old, and all his days were fifty-two years. <b>[לעלם — for a long time.]</b>"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:35.284340370Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}